Same assumptions as before: Node/TypeScript, Node 18+ for global `fetch`, and stubs where it touches your code. It uses the HaveIBeenPwned Pwned Passwords range API with k-anonymity, so only the first 5 hex characters of the SHA-1 hash leave your servers.

```ts
// breachCheck.ts
import { createHash } from "node:crypto";

const HIBP_RANGE_URL = "https://api.pwnedpasswords.com/range/";
const TIMEOUT_MS = 1500;
const CACHE_TTL_MS = 60 * 60 * 1000;
const CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES = 5000;

export type BreachResult =
  | { status: "breached"; count: number }
  | { status: "clean" }
  | { status: "unknown" }; // API unreachable, timed out, or returned an error

// Cache range responses by 5-char prefix. Swap for Redis if you run multiple instances.
const cache = new Map<string, { expires: number; suffixes: Map<string, number> }>();

function cacheGet(prefix: string) {
  const hit = cache.get(prefix);
  if (!hit) return null;
  if (hit.expires < Date.now()) {
    cache.delete(prefix);
    return null;
  }
  return hit.suffixes;
}

function cacheSet(prefix: string, suffixes: Map<string, number>) {
  if (cache.size >= CACHE_MAX_ENTRIES) {
    cache.delete(cache.keys().next().value!); // evict oldest insertion
  }
  cache.set(prefix, { expires: Date.now() + CACHE_TTL_MS, suffixes });
}

export async function checkPasswordBreached(password: string): Promise<BreachResult> {
  const sha1 = createHash("sha1").update(password, "utf8").digest("hex").toUpperCase();
  const prefix = sha1.slice(0, 5);
  const suffix = sha1.slice(5);

  let suffixes = cacheGet(prefix);

  if (!suffixes) {
    try {
      const resp = await fetch(HIBP_RANGE_URL + prefix, {
        headers: {
          "Add-Padding": "true", // pads responses so size doesn't leak how many matches exist
          "User-Agent": "YourApp-password-check",
        },
        signal: AbortSignal.timeout(TIMEOUT_MS),
      });
      if (!resp.ok) return { status: "unknown" };

      suffixes = new Map();
      for (const line of (await resp.text()).split("\n")) {
        const [s, c] = line.trim().split(":");
        const count = Number(c);
        if (s && count > 0) suffixes.set(s, count); // padding rows have count 0, so skip them
      }
      cacheSet(prefix, suffixes);
    } catch {
      return { status: "unknown" };
    }
  }

  const count = suffixes.get(suffix);
  return count ? { status: "breached", count } : { status: "clean" };
}
```

**Policy validator for signup and password change**

```ts
// passwordPolicy.ts
import { checkPasswordBreached } from "./breachCheck";

const MIN_LENGTH = 8;     // NIST 800-63B minimum; consider 10-12
const MAX_LENGTH = 128;   // bounds hashing cost; don't truncate silently

export type PolicyResult =
  | { ok: true }
  | { ok: false; reason: "too_short" | "too_long" | "breached" };

export async function validateNewPassword(password: string): Promise<PolicyResult> {
  if (password.length < MIN_LENGTH) return { ok: false, reason: "too_short" };
  if (password.length > MAX_LENGTH) return { ok: false, reason: "too_long" };

  const result = await checkPasswordBreached(password);
  if (result.status === "breached") return { ok: false, reason: "breached" };

  if (result.status === "unknown") {
    // Fail open so an HIBP outage doesn't block signups, but make it visible.
    metrics.increment("password_breach_check.unavailable");
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
declare const metrics: { increment(name: string): void };
```

The user-facing message for `breached` should explain, not just refuse: *"This password has appeared in a data breach at another site. Please choose a different one."* Skip composition rules (symbols, uppercase), because they add friction without much security. The breach check and length do the useful work.

**Login-time check (the part that matters for your incident)**

You can only see the plaintext password during login, so this is the only way to find existing users who reuse breached passwords. Run it after the password has been verified:

```ts
// inside your login handler, after verifying the password hash
const breach = await checkPasswordBreached(password);

if (breach.status === "breached") {
  await db.users.update(user.id, { password_breach_flagged_at: new Date() });

  // Don't complete the login. Anyone who knows this password, including the attacker, just passed.
  // Send a reset link to the account's email on file instead of letting this session through.
  await sendPasswordResetEmail(user.id);
  await revokeAllSessions(user.id);
  return res.status(403).json({ error: "password_reset_required" });
}
// "unknown" -> let the login proceed and never block logins on a third-party outage
```

Matching migration:

```sql
ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN password_breach_flagged_at TIMESTAMPTZ NULL;
```

**Things to be aware of**
- **The check doesn't evict attackers by itself.** Attackers using valid breached credentials will trigger this too, which is good. But the real owner is protected only if the reset goes to their email, not through the session that just authenticated. This is why MFA is the more important control. Run both.
- **Handle the rollout carefully.** Turning on the login-time block affects every user with a breached password at once, which could generate a support spike and a flood of reset emails. Consider a staged rollout, or force MFA enrollment for flagged users instead of a hard reset at first.
- **Known-affected accounts shouldn't wait.** For the accounts you've already identified, force a reset and revoke sessions now rather than waiting for their next login.
- **Never log the password or its full hash.** Make sure request-logging middleware excludes the login and password-change routes.
- **Rate-limit by account.** Credential stuffing with valid passwords won't trip per-IP limits. Add per-account anomaly signals (new device, new ASN or country, impossible travel) to trigger step-up verification.
- **Offline alternative.** If you don't want a runtime dependency on a third party, HIBP publishes a downloadable hash list (sorted by hash, so you can binary-search it or load it into a Bloom filter or Redis). The tradeoff is that you have to keep it updated.
- **Test it.** `password` should return `breached` with a very large count. Also test with the network blocked to confirm the `unknown` path behaves as intended.

