On ESP-IDF with deep sleep there is no `while(1)` loop. Each wake restarts `app_main()`, so one pass through it is one cycle. I'm still assuming an ESP32, since you haven't said otherwise.

```c
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <time.h>
#include "esp_sleep.h"
#include "esp_timer.h"
#include "esp_attr.h"

#define SAMPLE_PERIOD_S      60
#define SAMPLES_PER_TX       10
#define CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS   8000    /* never let a dead AP burn 80+ mA indefinitely */
#define MAX_BACKOFF_CYCLES   30      /* cap retry spacing; keep < buffer capacity */

/* Survives deep sleep */
static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint8_t s_fail_streak;
static RTC_DATA_ATTR uint8_t s_retry_wait;   /* sample cycles to skip before next TX attempt */

/* Returns true only if every buffered reading was sent. */
static bool try_transmit(void)
{
    uint16_t sent = 0;
    uint16_t total = readings_count();
    reading_t r;

    if (radio_connect_timeout(CONNECT_TIMEOUT_MS) == ESP_OK) {
        while (readings_get(sent, &r) && radio_send(&r) == ESP_OK) {
            sent++;
        }
        radio_disconnect();           /* stops the radio, not just the link */
    }
    readings_discard(sent);
    return sent == total;
}

void app_main(void)
{
    int64_t t_start_us = esp_timer_get_time();

    readings_init();

    /* 1. Sample (radio is off). Power the sensor from a GPIO if you can,
          and turn it off again right after the read. */
    readings_add((uint32_t)time(NULL), read_sensor_scaled());

    /* 2. Transmit only when due, with backoff after failures. */
    if (s_retry_wait > 0) {
        s_retry_wait--;
    } else if (readings_count() >= SAMPLES_PER_TX) {
        if (try_transmit()) {
            s_fail_streak = 0;
        } else {
            if (s_fail_streak < 8) s_fail_streak++;
            uint32_t wait = 1U << s_fail_streak;
            s_retry_wait = (wait > MAX_BACKOFF_CYCLES) ? MAX_BACKOFF_CYCLES : wait;
        }
    }

    /* 3. Sleep for the remainder of the period, so a long TX doesn't
          stretch the sample interval. */
    uint32_t active_s = (uint32_t)((esp_timer_get_time() - t_start_us) / 1000000) + 1;
    uint32_t sleep_s  = (active_s < SAMPLE_PERIOD_S) ? SAMPLE_PERIOD_S - active_s : 1;

    ESP_ERROR_CHECK(set_wake_timer(sleep_s));
    esp_deep_sleep_start();           /* does not return */
}
```

The cycle works like this:

1. The timer fires and the chip resets.
2. `readings_init()` keeps the RTC buffer if it's valid.
3. It takes a sample with the radio off.
4. On most wakes it goes straight back to sleep. Every `SAMPLES_PER_TX` wakes it connects, sends, and disconnects.
5. If the connection fails, it skips the next 2, 4, 8, ... cycles (capped at 30) instead of retrying every minute at full current. Readings stay buffered. At one sample per minute, 128 slots last about 2 hours before the oldest entries are overwritten, so keep the backoff cap well under that.

I need to correct something I said earlier. I said a sample-only wake takes "tens of milliseconds." On an ESP32 the bootloader and startup usually take about 100-300 ms at tens of mA, even with the radio off. A rough budget with my guessed numbers:

| Phase | Estimate | Average |
|---|---|---|
| Sample wake | ~0.2 s × 40 mA every 60 s | ~0.13 mA |
| Transmit | ~4 s × 100 mA every 10 min | ~0.67 mA |
| Deep sleep | board-dependent | ~0.01 mA or more |
| **Total** | | **~0.8 mA** |

Against the roughly 5.8 Ah battery implied by your three-day figure, that's about 300 days. It clears six months, but not by a huge margin, and these numbers are guesses. Transmission dominates, so the biggest levers are:

- Transmitting less often.
- Shortening connect time (static IP, cached channel and BSSID).
- Using a lighter protocol.

Measure a real cycle with a current profiler or a shunt and scope before trusting the estimate.

Things that can still sink the budget:

- **Sleep current of the whole board.** A dev board's USB-serial chip and regulator can draw several mA by themselves. Measure on your actual hardware.
- **Sensor power.** A sensor left powered continuously can eat the entire sleep budget.
- **Brownout resets.** Radio current spikes on a weak or cold battery can reset the chip. That wipes the RTC buffer, since `readings_init()` treats it as a fresh boot.
- **Clock sync.** `time()` starts at 0 after a power-on. Sync SNTP on first boot and occasionally afterward, or timestamps will be meaningless.

If you tell me the actual MCU, radio, and battery, I can redo this with real numbers.

