You didn't mention your hardware, so I've assumed an ESP32 (ESP-IDF or Arduino-ESP32), since it's common for field sensors. If you're on something else, tell me and I'll rewrite it.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include "esp_sleep.h"
#include "esp_err.h"

#define US_PER_SEC        1000000ULL
#define MAX_SLEEP_SEC     (60UL * 60UL * 24UL)   // sanity cap: 24 h, adjust as needed

/**
 * Arm the timer that will wake the device from sleep.
 *
 * @param seconds  Time until wake, in seconds (1..MAX_SLEEP_SEC).
 * @return ESP_OK on success, ESP_ERR_INVALID_ARG if out of range.
 *
 * Call this BEFORE entering light/deep sleep. It only arms the timer;
 * it does not put the device to sleep.
 */
esp_err_t set_wake_timer(uint32_t seconds)
{
    if (seconds == 0 || seconds > MAX_SLEEP_SEC) {
        return ESP_ERR_INVALID_ARG;
    }

    uint64_t sleep_us = (uint64_t)seconds * US_PER_SEC;  // cast avoids 32-bit overflow
    return esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(sleep_us);
}
```

Example usage:

```c
void sleep_until_next_transmission(void)
{
    radio_sleep();                         // your radio driver's sleep call
    if (set_wake_timer(300) == ESP_OK) {   // wake in 5 minutes
        esp_deep_sleep_start();            // or esp_light_sleep_start()
    }
}
```

Some things to keep in mind for battery life:

- **Putting only the radio to sleep may not be enough.** If the MCU stays awake in an active or idle loop, it may draw more than the radio saves. Deep sleep for the whole device, with the radio asleep too, usually gives the biggest gain.
- **Deep sleep resets the CPU.** Execution restarts from `main`/`app_main` on wake, so anything you need to keep (sequence numbers, calibration, etc.) must go in RTC memory (`RTC_DATA_ATTR`) or flash/NVS. Light sleep preserves RAM but draws more current.
- **Timer accuracy.** The ESP32's internal RTC oscillator drifts by a few percent with temperature. If you need precise timing, for example to sync with a gateway's receive window, consider an external 32 kHz crystal or an external RTC.
- **Measure it.** Check actual sleep current with a power profiler or a good multimeter. Leaky peripherals such as sensor power rails, pull-ups, and LEDs often dominate once the radio is off.

If you tell me the MCU, radio module, and how often you transmit, I can write the sleep/wake sequence around this function and help estimate battery life.

This buffer is placed in RTC memory (`RTC_DATA_ATTR`), not ordinary RAM. If you use deep sleep, regular RAM is wiped on every wake, so a normal static array would lose your readings each cycle. RTC memory survives deep sleep and costs only a little extra sleep current.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include <stddef.h>
#include "esp_attr.h"

/* Adjust this struct to match your sensor. */
typedef struct {
    uint32_t timestamp;          // seconds (epoch or since first boot)
    int16_t  temperature_c_x100; // e.g. 2345 = 23.45 C
    uint16_t humidity_x100;
    uint16_t battery_mv;
} reading_t;

#define READING_BUF_CAPACITY  64          // 64 * 12 bytes = 768 B; RTC slow mem is ~8 KB on ESP32
#define READING_BUF_MAGIC     0xB0FFE125u

typedef struct {
    uint32_t  magic;
    uint16_t  head;      // index of oldest reading
    uint16_t  count;     // number of valid readings
    uint32_t  dropped;   // readings overwritten due to overflow (diagnostics)
    reading_t data[READING_BUF_CAPACITY];
} reading_buf_t;

RTC_DATA_ATTR static reading_buf_t s_buf;

/* Call once at startup. Only resets the buffer if RTC memory is not valid
 * (first power-up, brownout, etc.); preserves contents across deep sleep. */
void reading_buffer_init(void)
{
    if (s_buf.magic != READING_BUF_MAGIC ||
        s_buf.head >= READING_BUF_CAPACITY ||
        s_buf.count > READING_BUF_CAPACITY) {
        s_buf.magic   = READING_BUF_MAGIC;
        s_buf.head    = 0;
        s_buf.count   = 0;
        s_buf.dropped = 0;
    }
}

/* Append a reading. If full, the OLDEST reading is overwritten.
 * Returns true if stored without loss, false if an old reading was dropped. */
bool reading_buffer_add(const reading_t *r)
{
    bool lossless = true;

    if (s_buf.count == READING_BUF_CAPACITY) {
        s_buf.head = (s_buf.head + 1) % READING_BUF_CAPACITY;  // drop oldest
        s_buf.count--;
        s_buf.dropped++;
        lossless = false;
    }

    uint16_t tail = (s_buf.head + s_buf.count) % READING_BUF_CAPACITY;
    s_buf.data[tail] = *r;
    s_buf.count++;
    return lossless;
}

size_t reading_buffer_count(void)
{
    return s_buf.count;
}

/* Copy up to max readings (oldest first) into out WITHOUT removing them.
 * Returns the number copied. */
size_t reading_buffer_peek(reading_t *out, size_t max)
{
    size_t n = (s_buf.count < max) ? s_buf.count : max;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        out[i] = s_buf.data[(s_buf.head + i) % READING_BUF_CAPACITY];
    }
    return n;
}

/* Remove the oldest n readings. Call this only after a transmission is confirmed. */
void reading_buffer_discard(size_t n)
{
    if (n > s_buf.count) n = s_buf.count;
    s_buf.head = (s_buf.head + n) % READING_BUF_CAPACITY;
    s_buf.count -= n;
}
```

Example wake cycle:

```c
void app_main(void)
{
    reading_buffer_init();

    reading_t r = read_sensors();            // your sensor code
    reading_buffer_add(&r);

    if (reading_buffer_count() >= 12) {      // e.g. 12 readings, then transmit
        reading_t batch[12];
        size_t n = reading_buffer_peek(batch, 12);

        radio_wake();
        if (radio_send(batch, n)) {          // your radio driver
            reading_buffer_discard(n);       // only drop data once it's delivered
        }
        radio_sleep();
    }

    if (set_wake_timer(300) == ESP_OK) {     // sample every 5 min
        esp_deep_sleep_start();
    }
}
```

Design notes:

- **Peek then discard.** If a transmission fails, the data stays buffered and goes out with the next attempt instead of being lost.
- **Overflow policy.** When the buffer fills, it overwrites the oldest data, since recent readings usually matter more. The `dropped` counter lets you report data loss. If you'd rather keep the oldest data, change `reading_buffer_add` to reject new readings when full.
- **Sizing.** Capacity should cover your transmit interval plus margin for failed sends. At one reading every 5 minutes, 64 entries is over 5 hours of data.
- **Power loss.** RTC memory is lost if the battery dies or the board browns out. If you can't afford to lose readings, periodically flush to NVS/flash, but do it sparingly because flash writes cost energy and wear the flash.
- **Concurrency.** This isn't thread-safe. In the single-task wake, sample, sleep pattern above that's fine. If you add multiple tasks or ISRs writing to it, wrap access in a mutex or critical section.
- **Timestamps.** After deep sleep, `esp_timer` restarts from zero. If you need continuous timestamps, use `time()`/`gettimeofday()`, which the RTC keeps running across deep sleep, or keep a sequence number in RTC memory.

