In deep sleep there is no loop. The CPU resets on every wake and runs `app_main` from the top, so each pass is one full cycle: wake, sample, maybe transmit, sleep. (On Arduino-ESP32, `setup()` plays this role and `loop()` never runs.) This sketch reuses `reading_buffer_*` and builds on `set_wake_timer`, but it computes the sleep time itself so the sampling cadence doesn't drift.

```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>
#include "esp_sleep.h"
#include "esp_timer.h"
#include "esp_attr.h"

#define SAMPLE_INTERVAL_S    300     // one reading every 5 min
#define TX_BATCH_SIZE        12      // transmit once per hour
#define TX_MAX_ATTEMPTS      3
#define TX_MAX_BACKOFF       8       // max cycles to skip after repeated failures
#define LOW_BATT_MV          3300    // below this, stop transmitting and stretch the interval
#define LOW_BATT_INTERVAL_S  (SAMPLE_INTERVAL_S * 4)

/* State that must survive deep sleep */
RTC_DATA_ATTR static uint8_t s_tx_failures    = 0;  // consecutive failed transmit cycles
RTC_DATA_ATTR static uint8_t s_tx_skip_cycles = 0;  // cycles left before the next attempt

static bool transmit_batch(void)
{
    reading_t batch[TX_BATCH_SIZE];
    size_t n = reading_buffer_peek(batch, TX_BATCH_SIZE);
    if (n == 0) return true;

    bool ok = false;
    radio_wake();
    for (int attempt = 0; attempt < TX_MAX_ATTEMPTS && !ok; attempt++) {
        ok = radio_send(batch, n);        // your driver; should have its own timeout
    }
    radio_sleep();                        // always sleep the radio, success or not

    if (ok) reading_buffer_discard(n);    // drop data only after confirmed delivery
    return ok;
}

void app_main(void)
{
    esp_sleep_wakeup_cause_t cause = esp_sleep_get_wakeup_cause();
    bool first_boot = (cause == ESP_SLEEP_WAKEUP_UNDEFINED);  // power-on or reset, not a timer wake

    reading_buffer_init();
    if (first_boot) {
        // One-time setup: set the clock, log the reset reason, etc.
    }

    /* 1. Sample */
    sensor_power_on();                    // switch the sensor rail on only while needed
    reading_t r = read_sensors();
    sensor_power_off();
    reading_buffer_add(&r);

    /* 2. Decide whether to transmit */
    bool battery_low = (r.battery_mv < LOW_BATT_MV);

    if (s_tx_skip_cycles > 0) {
        s_tx_skip_cycles--;               // backing off after earlier failures
    } else if (!battery_low && reading_buffer_count() >= TX_BATCH_SIZE) {
        if (transmit_batch()) {
            s_tx_failures = 0;
        } else {
            if (s_tx_failures < TX_MAX_BACKOFF) s_tx_failures++;
            s_tx_skip_cycles = s_tx_failures; // wait 1, 2, 3... cycles before retrying
        }
    }

    /* 3. Sleep, compensating for time spent awake */
    uint32_t interval_s = battery_low ? LOW_BATT_INTERVAL_S : SAMPLE_INTERVAL_S;
    uint64_t interval_us = (uint64_t)interval_s * 1000000ULL;
    uint64_t awake_us = (uint64_t)esp_timer_get_time();   // time since this wake
    uint64_t sleep_us = (awake_us < interval_us) ? (interval_us - awake_us) : 1000ULL;

    esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(sleep_us);
    esp_deep_sleep_start();               // does not return
}
```

How it behaves:

- **Cadence compensation.** A cycle that includes a transmission may keep the device awake for seconds. Subtracting the awake time keeps samples evenly spaced instead of stretching them by the transmit time.
- **Retry with backoff.** A failed send leaves the data buffered. Each consecutive failure skips more cycles before the next attempt, up to `TX_MAX_BACKOFF`. This matters because the radio is your biggest energy cost, and an out-of-range node retrying every cycle can drain the battery fast. Readings keep accumulating in the meantime, and the buffer's overflow policy covers the worst case.
- **Low-battery mode.** Below the threshold, the node keeps sampling but stops transmitting and slows down. It stays alive longer and the data is still there if the battery recovers (for example, after a cold night or solar charging).
- **Radio always sleeps.** `radio_sleep()` runs on both the success and failure paths, so a failed send can't leave it powered.

Things to check when you adapt this:

- **GPIO state in deep sleep.** Pins controlling the sensor rail, the radio's enable or chip-select, and anything with pull-ups can float or leak current during sleep. Use `gpio_hold_en()` and `gpio_deep_sleep_hold_en()` to lock them at known levels.
- **Awake time dominates the average.** A 2-second transmit at 120 mA costs far more than 5 minutes of sleep at 10 µA. Measure the awake duration of each phase (sensor warm-up, radio join, send) and optimize the longest first.
- **Watchdog.** If a driver call can hang, such as a radio waiting on a gateway, a stuck node will drain the battery while awake. Give the radio calls hard timeouts, and consider enabling the task watchdog so a hang causes a reset instead of a dead node.
- **Battery reading under load.** Voltage sags during transmit. Read `battery_mv` before the radio turns on (as above), or you'll trigger low-battery mode falsely.

If you tell me the radio type (LoRa, Wi-Fi, cellular, BLE) I can fill in `radio_wake`/`radio_send`/`radio_sleep` properly. Join and handshake costs differ a lot between them and often decide the optimal batch size.

